        "id": 132717,
        "name": "Nataraja",
        "username": "Nataraja",
        "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/n/7feea3/{size}.png",
        "created_at": "2015-02-21T02:12:44.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"Donner\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/donner/48/1903_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Donner:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>There is a difference between a target entering play and a card entering play.&nbsp; When a card becomes a target, the target (being the card is now targetable) enters play then.&nbsp; Likewise, when a card that was targetable becomes untargetable, that target leaves play.&nbsp; Similar effects occur in Magic: The Gathering and other card games.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>Actually, no. In Magic when a permanent changes state it does not \"enter play\" as&nbsp; the new state. One of the oldest and clearest examples is Mishra's Workshop: when you activate its ability to become a creature, it does not enter play as a creature, thus not triggering a number of effect depending on a creature/permanent to enter play</p>\n<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"eugeneruss\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/e/a183cd/48.png\" class=\"avatar\"> eugeneruss:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Willing to bet other card games with similar effects use better language. If not, hey, there's a reason I gave up Magic beyond just the need to buy insane amounts of card, and that reason was the endless errata and clarifications.</p>\n<p>It's not so odd with a card flip. It's odd when it happens with transforming a card that hasn't flipped. Nothing entered the board, something just became vulnerable. And if similar effects triggered off of Caspit's, I'm afraid I'd have to guffaw over the idea that all those new pieces of equipment had only just entered play.</p>\n<p>This triggers my inherent dislike for ugly mechanics. I like the mechanics to reflect what's going on, and no target appeared, reality just got warped. No need to repeat the sentence too. I get the ruling and the thinking. I just find it counter-intuitive and inelegant.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>Actually Magic needs very very little errata. Clarifictions? Maybe, but I always found that a thorough reading of the Comprehensive Rules gives a remarkable insight into the game.</p>\n<p>Clarifications in Magic make me react less \"WTF I never would have thought that\" and more \"Oh, that's right, how silly of me to not get that by myself\". M:tG's comprehensive rules manages to keep coherent a game with roughly a thousand new cards printed every year, as such it's like a work of art in the form of a rulebook to me.</p>",
